   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/2Pq4/8/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "dxc5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Capturing on c5 is double-edged: it allows Black to trade queens and drag White's king into the centre. Many players avoid it for that reason, but the resulting queenless middlegame is fully playable.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "A capture that trades queens and exposes your king isn't bad — just know the endgame is the real battle.",
   "plans": "Accept the king walk; the extra structure compensates."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 127.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/2P5/8/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBK1B1R b kq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "Qxd1+",
    "Kxd1"
   ],
   "spoken": "After the queens come off and the king recaptures on d1, the position turns sharp and technical — a queenless middlegame where accurate play, not memorised theory, decides.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "A king in the centre is fine with queens off — activity and accuracy matter more than castling.",
   "plans": "Activate the pieces and hold the extra pawn."
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 134,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/p1P5/8/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBK1B1R w kq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "a5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
